-\item Let $\text{u}=\langle u_{1},u_{2}\rangle$ and $\text{v}=\langle v_{1},v_{2}\rangle$ be two-dimensional vectors. The cross product of vectors $\text{u}$ and $\text{v}$ is not defined. However, if the vectors are regarded as the three-dimensional vectors $\tilde{\text{u}}=\langle u_{1},u_{2},0\rangle$ and $\tilde{\text{v}}=\langle v_{1},v_{2},0\rangle$, respectively, then, in this case, we can define the cross product of $\tilde{\text{u}}$ and $\tilde{\text{v}}$. In particular, in determinant notation, the cross product of $\tilde{\text{u}}$ and $\tilde{\text{v}}$ is given by \[\tilde{\text{u}}\times \tilde{\text{v}}=|\begin{matrix}\text{i} & \text{j} & \text{k} \\ u_{1} & u_{2} & 0 \\ v_{1} & v_{2} & 0\end{matrix}|.\] Use this result to compute $(\text{i}\cos \theta+\text{j}\sin \theta)\times (\text{i}\sin \theta-\text{j}\cos \theta)$, where $\theta$ is a real number.
+\item Show that vectors $\mathbf{i}+\mathbf{j}$, $\mathbf{i}-\mathbf{j}$, and $\mathbf{i}+\mathbf{j}+\mathbf{k}$ are linearly independent---that is, there do not exist two nonzero real numbers $\alpha$ and $\beta$ such that $\mathbf{i}+\mathbf{j}+\mathbf{k}=\alpha(\mathbf{i}+\mathbf{j})+\beta(\mathbf{i}-\mathbf{j})$.
